00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.190
And then the other portion, that
managed service portion that

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.430
lives within Google Cloud,
remain Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.500
So just so you have that
context as I talk about things,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.110
you'll know what
I'm talking about.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.310
So what do we mean
by a Unified Model?

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.480
So the beginnings of the
need for this Unified Model

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.480
really can trace
back to early 2011.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.000
So in 2011 time frame,
stream processing

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.310
was just starting
to become a thing.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.180
And one of the things that
happened was stream processing

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.490
systems at the time,
like Apache Storm,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.940
was that they made
design decisions in order

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.330
to reduce latency.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.830
That also reduced their ability
to provide correct results.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.950
And so Nathan Marz, who wrote
this blog post, who is also

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.430
the creator of Apache Storm,
came up with this idea

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:36.490
that we'd like to have low
latency results of our system,

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.130
but we also want
them to be correct.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.070
And our streaming systems
can't give us correctness.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.814
So instead, why don't
we do this thing

00:03:42.814 --> 00:03:44.730
on there called the
Lambda Architecture, which

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.910
is you've got your data
set on the left there

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.160
and you run it through some
sort of stream processing

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.020
system that gives you low
latency, but not really

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.180
correct, results like
Apache Storm on the bottom--

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.480
just calling it out because
it's Nathan's system.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.210
And then you run it also through
a batch processing system

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.720
that gives you strong
consistency, like Apache

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.297
Hadoop on top.

00:04:05.297 --> 00:04:07.380
You somehow merge the
results together at the end,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.380
and you end up getting
what you want, right?

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:10.830
You want these low
latency results

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.370
that are eventually correct.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.459
The downside is that you're
building and maintaining

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:19.170
and then somehow
merging the results

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:20.310
from two separate systems.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.980
This is costly among
a ton of dimensions.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.050
And so one of the
big goals we set out

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.840
to have with Cloud
Dataflow and then

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.080
Apache Beam was to come
up with this Unified

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.070
Model for doing this.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.380
So let you write
your pipeline one

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.440
time in one way that lets you
take your data set and get

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.882
your low latency, but also
eventually correct, results.

00:04:41.882 --> 00:04:43.590
But what also falls
out from this is then

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.560
once you've established
this system,

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.014
it also allows you to
choose the tradeoffs

00:04:49.014 --> 00:04:49.930
that you want to make.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.350
So if you're OK
in this situation,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.510
using a batch processing
engine on the back end

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.510
and trading off some of that
latency for greater efficiency,

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.870
you can do that.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.080
And so what you have is
you've got your Beam pipeline

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:03.350
you've written.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.240
And you've got to run
it on something, right?

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.444
And so you can run
it on a batch system.

00:05:07.444 --> 00:05:09.110
Or you can run it on
a streaming system.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.250
The choice is up to you.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.374
The big point is that you
get to make this informed

00:05:12.374 --> 00:05:14.210
decision that lets
you know trade off

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.575
these tensions between balancing
correctness latency and cost.

00:05:18.575 --> 00:05:21.080
And if you want to learn more
about the details of this,

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.950
we published a paper on
this at VLDB in 2013.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:27.410
And so you can go look this
up, and read through it.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.160
And see both the details
of the underlying model

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.640
and really the conceptual
motivations behind it.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:36.830
But it's not just
about this trading off

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.130
between these different
tensions that really

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:41.360
motivated us to create Beam.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:42.818
There's another
aspect of it that's

00:05:42.818 --> 00:05:44.270
really important as well.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:48.380
And that is this ability to
run your pipeline, really,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:49.790
on any supported runner.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.100
And so it's not just batch
or streaming, but really

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.460
the choice of whatever
execution engine is the best

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.075
for your specific use case.

00:05:57.075 --> 00:05:59.450
And then, of course, you're
all here at this Google Cloud

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:00.680
conference.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.410
Our goal, as Google Cloud
developer, or Google Cloud

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.720
Dataflow developers, is
to make Cloud Dataflow

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:07.910
the absolute best place
for you to go and run

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:10.660
your Beam pipelines.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.920
Beam allows you to
have this open source

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.140
way of accessing Cloud
Dataflow that doesn't lock you

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:15.920
into Google Cloud.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.300
It's that open cloud
motivation there.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.550
And so you can come to Google.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:20.966
You've written
your Beam pipeline.

00:06:20.966 --> 00:06:23.449
You hand it off to
us in Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.490
And we run it through our
optimizer that comes up

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.650
with the best way
to run it possibly,

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.070
schedule [INAUDIBLE]
machines, brings up VMs,

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.310
integrates cleanly with all the
various portions of the Google

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:34.810
Cloud.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.184
And all of this is done within
the managed service, where

00:06:37.184 --> 00:06:38.870
you don't have to do
any of the ops work

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.000
to actually keep all
this up and running.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.070
So that's the rough introduction
to what we meant by the Unified

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.020
Model and where it came from.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.490
So next, let's look at
infinite, out-of-order data

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:53.690
sets, because these were
really the motivating reason

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:56.210
for needing this Unified Model.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:01.280
So in order to do this, some
ground things in a concept--

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:02.610
a concrete example.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.820
So imagine you are some startup.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.100
You've created some
new cool mobile game

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.590
where users go on their
phones, and they crush candy

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:11.720
or kill orcs or whatever.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.330
And as they do this,
you're collecting logs

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:15.710
about the things they're doing.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.180
And through our example,
the biggest thing

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.638
is we're going to say, we're
collecting user scores, right?

00:07:19.638 --> 00:07:22.700
And we want to be able to tally
up user scores and team scores

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.370
and basically analyze the
data and understand how

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.090
people are using our product.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.625
So we start out with a data set.

00:07:29.625 --> 00:07:32.340
So this is representing the
logs from our games, right?

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.020
And each of these little
squares is one user score.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.990
And as our game
gets more popular,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.260
our data set may get bigger.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.007
And eventually it may
get so big that we

00:07:44.007 --> 00:07:46.340
started organizing into some
sort of repeated structure,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:46.839
right?

00:07:46.839 --> 00:07:49.290
We start collecting
logs for a given day,

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.680
so Tuesdays logs
and Wednesdays logs.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.500
But really this is
just a cheap way

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.560
of representing an
infinite data source.

00:07:59.309 --> 00:08:01.100
The game logs are
continuous, so really you

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.320
want to treat them in
a continuous manner.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.750
What's worse, because we're
dealing with the service

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.960
systems here, and we're
dealing with mobile devices,

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:10.380
there's ambiguity involved.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.110
So let's look at a
few of these scores.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.110
So we're going look at
three different scores

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:17.930
from different gamers that
all happened conceptually

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:18.560
at 8:00 AM.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.660
So all of them were on
their phone at 8:00 AM

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:23.880
and made these scores.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.370
So this first one, this red
one here, happened at 8:00 AM.

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:30.069
And you can see within our big,
unbounded, steam of data here,

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:31.610
it showed up pretty
close to 8:00 AM.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.539
So that's great.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:35.990
And then this yellow one,
it was delayed a little bit,

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:39.890
but it also showed up
roughly close to 8:00 AM.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.070
But then somebody
representing this green square

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.020
was on a plane or
something in airplane mode,

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.090
and they'd scored
the score at 8:00

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.500
but it wasn't until
whatever that is-- five,

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.950
six hours later-- that they
actually took the phone out

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.622
of airplane mode and were
able to upload that score.

00:08:54.622 --> 00:08:57.080
So this is what I mean when we
talk about out-of-order data

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.270
sets.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.584
Conceptually, that score
happened back a lot closer

00:09:01.584 --> 00:09:02.750
to where these two happened.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.140
But we didn't learn about
it until hours later.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.120
So now we got this
infinite unbounded

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.190
out-of-order data set.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:11.210
How do we process it?

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.710
Well, there are a
few different ways

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:15.211
that you can approach
this depending

00:09:15.211 --> 00:09:16.460
on what your pipeline's doing.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:18.350
So the most simple,
simple kind are

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.850
element-wise transformations.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.640
So with element-wise
transformations,

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.976
you're just doing things on
an element by element basis.

00:09:24.976 --> 00:09:27.350
So in this case, we're basically
filtering out everything

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:29.194
except for the yellow elements.

00:09:29.194 --> 00:09:30.860
You don't have to
look at anything other

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.526
than a single element
at any given time.

00:09:32.526 --> 00:09:36.090
So it's relatively
straightforward.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.720
Another way to do
this, once you start

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.220
wanting to do an
aggregation, you've

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.120
got to group your elements
together somehow over time.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.870
So one way to do this
is to aggregate them

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.078
into processing time windows.

00:09:46.078 --> 00:09:48.290
So you just basically
break apart time

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:51.362
as elements arrive as they're
observed in the stream,

00:09:51.362 --> 00:09:52.820
and say, this is
my window of time.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:56.180
I'm going to treat this as a
group of elements together.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:57.625
It's the simplest way to do it.

00:09:57.625 --> 00:09:59.750
But it also means if your
elements are arriving out

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.442
of order, they may be processed
within groups that they

00:10:02.442 --> 00:10:03.400
don't really relate to.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.441
So if you remember that
green element we had that

00:10:05.441 --> 00:10:08.850
showed up way more, like 1
o'clock in the afternoon,

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.110
we would account that with
all these other events that

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.150
happened around 1:00
instead of with events

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.070
that happened around
8:00 like we should have.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.920
So what we really
want when you're

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.130
dealing with out-of-order
data is event-time windowings.

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:24.340
So event-time windowing lets
you take this unordered steam

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:26.800
of data and, effectively,
perform a time-based shuffle

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.860
to put these elements that
arrive out of order back

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.320
into the order they should
have been and aggregate them

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.800
together with the elements
that they belong with.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.930
But to do this, we
really need to formalize

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:43.960
the relationship between
processing time and event time.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:45.622
So what we have here is a graph.

00:10:45.622 --> 00:10:47.830
And we're going to actually
look at a bunch of graphs

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:49.370
throughout this presentation.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:53.230
But on the x-axis,
we have event time.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:54.139
So that's in blue.

00:10:54.139 --> 00:10:55.930
So this is when the
events actually happen.

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:57.971
So the user's on their
phone, playing their game,

00:10:57.971 --> 00:11:02.580
and they scored some points,
and they were recorded.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:04.360
That's the event time.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:06.837
The processing time is,
then, when the events show up

00:11:06.837 --> 00:11:07.420
in the system.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:08.961
We learn about them
and process them.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.810
And that dash line up the
middle would be the ideal.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.472
If events happened were
processed at the same time,

00:11:16.472 --> 00:11:17.680
they'd all lie on that ideal.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:20.470
But reality is a little
more like this red curve.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.780
Because of distributed
systems, because of delays

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:27.400
and for mobile
phones, et cetera,

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:29.980
you get this wandering,
meandering line

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:31.630
that sometimes comes
close to the ideal

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:34.960
but oftentimes
skews away from it.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.800
And really, this horizontal
distance between the ideal

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.340
and this red line, this
reference the skew and event

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:42.870
time.

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.970
So how far off are we
from the ideal as far as

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.940
completeness and event time?

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:53.260
And in order to reason about
completeness and correctness,

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:57.050
we need to be able to
understand where that skew is.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:11:59.392
And so we do this was a
thing called a watermark.

00:11:59.392 --> 00:12:00.850
And a watermark,
essentially, tries

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:02.650
to capture the notion
of this red line.

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:06.100
It describes progress of
event time completeness

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:06.870
within the system.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.850
It's roughly a guarantee
that no time stamp

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:12.256
earlier than the watermark
will ever be seen again.

00:12:12.256 --> 00:12:14.380
Now, you can actually have
two types of watermarks,

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.189
depending on what
your input source is.

00:12:17.189 --> 00:12:19.480
If you have complete knowledge
about your input source,

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:21.970
you're actually able to provide
a perfect watermark, which

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:23.920
means this literally
is a guarantee.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:27.070
You will never see a time stamp
earlier than the watermark.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:30.010
Because we deal with
distributed systems or systems,

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:33.520
like in our case where
we are reading from--

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:37.370
accepting mobile
phone scores, we

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:40.150
have no way of knowing all
the mobile phones out there

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:43.640
that might be having scores
made on them at a given time

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:44.830
and then uploaded later.

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.871
In that case, you often
have to create watermarks

00:12:46.871 --> 00:12:48.790
that are heuristic-based.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:53.590
So you take all the information
and make the best estimate you

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:54.940
can of what the watermark is.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:58.124
And then, in that case,
you may actually be wrong,

00:12:58.124 --> 00:12:59.540
and you'll have
to deal with that.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:01.671
So there's two problems,
then, that are listed here

00:13:01.671 --> 00:13:02.920
at the bottom with watermarks.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:04.592
One is that they
can be too slow.

00:13:04.592 --> 00:13:06.550
If you're accurately
holding back the watermark

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.860
because you know there's
late data, that's fine.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:09.719
But it also is--

00:13:09.719 --> 00:13:12.010
you're essentially holding
back progress of the system,

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.585
waiting for these records
that you know are late.

00:13:14.585 --> 00:13:16.210
The other is that
they can be too fast.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:18.130
So the case of
having a heuristic

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.470
watermark, if your
heuristic is wrong,

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:22.690
if your guess is
off, you'll say,

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.610
oh yeah, the watermark has
advanced to time x but stuff

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.940
with time stamp before
x may show up later.

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:29.740
So that's a case of
it being too late.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:33.180
So we'll look at ways that we
address this in the future.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:36.310
But that leads us to
this next section.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:37.869
So how do we deal
with these issues?

00:13:37.869 --> 00:13:39.910
Well, it's not quite so
hard if you actually know

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:42.400
what kinds of questions to ask.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:46.430
So what we've done with
Apache Beam and Cloud Dataflow

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:50.170
is try to provide a system that
lets you think about your data

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:53.530
processing problem by answering
these four simple questions.

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:55.450
So what is it that
you're computing?

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.560
Are you creating sums,
or are you doing joins,

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:00.130
or are you building histograms,
machine learning models?

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:03.970
This is the question that you've
traditionally probably thought

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.605
about answering with
classic batch processing.

00:14:06.605 --> 00:14:08.230
But beyond that, when
you want to start

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.344
dealing with these
unbounded data sets,

00:14:10.344 --> 00:14:12.760
there's a few other questions
you want to start answering.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:14.860
So where in event time are
you actually performing

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:15.790
these calculations?

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:19.030
Because when you have this
infinite, unbounded data set,

00:14:19.030 --> 00:14:20.406
you can't just
assume that you'll

00:14:20.406 --> 00:14:22.905
wait till you see the end of
it because there may be no end.

00:14:22.905 --> 00:14:24.760
You're going to have
to chop it up some way.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.860
And then after
you've defined where

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:29.920
an event time this is happening,
when in processing time

00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:31.810
do you actually
emit results for it?

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:35.020
Do you wait until you think
your inputs are complete?

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.610
Do you want to give speculative
results over time that

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.370
show your answers evolving?

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:41.240
And, in cases where
you have late data,

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:42.840
what do you do if
data show up late?

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:43.330
Do you throw them away?

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:44.650
Do you incorporate them in?

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.330
How do you deal with this?

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:48.760
And lastly then, in
cases where you do

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:51.730
provide multiple results,
how do those refinements

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:54.740
of those results
relate over time?

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:57.040
So we're going to go
now through examples

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:00.579
of each of those questions and
see what I mean in more detail.

00:15:00.579 --> 00:15:02.620
So for the what question,
what are you computing,

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:04.210
we talked previously
about there's

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:06.725
element-wise operations.

00:15:06.725 --> 00:15:07.600
There's aggregations.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:10.570
There's also this notion within
Beam of composite operations.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:14.230
So taking a set of more
primitive operations

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:16.330
and turning them into
a higher-level concept,

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:19.870
like a sum, right, which is a
grouping and then an addition

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:22.420
operation, or deduction.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:25.640
So, for our example, we're going
to look through some code here.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.660
This is pseudo Java because
it needs to fit on slides.

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:31.720
And we're going to elide
a lot of this as we go.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:34.369
The basic idea is we're
reading some log lines.

00:15:34.369 --> 00:15:35.410
So that's the first line.

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:36.993
We're reading them
into a PCollection.

00:15:36.993 --> 00:15:39.040
The PCollection is
the Beam and Dataflow

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:43.070
notion of a massive, potentially
massive, collection of data.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.320
Then we're going to apply
these transformations to it.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.195
So first we're going to
apply a parser that's

00:15:47.195 --> 00:15:50.789
going to parse it into this
key-value pairs of strings

00:15:50.789 --> 00:15:51.330
and integers.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:52.960
So users are going to
be team-score pairs

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:55.300
because we're going to want
to compute team scores here.

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:56.883
And then, lastly,
we're going to apply

00:15:56.883 --> 00:15:59.170
this composite
Sum.integersPerKey

00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:00.910
operation, which is
just going to compute

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:04.830
these per-team scores for us.

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.790
And so for each of these
examples, we're going to have,

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:11.710
again, an event time on
the x-axis, green y-axis,

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:13.697
processing time graph.

00:16:13.697 --> 00:16:16.030
And each of these circles
here represents a single score

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:17.530
from a single user
on the same team.

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:19.150
We're looking at
one team at a time,

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:21.358
just because otherwise it
would be three-dimensional,

00:16:21.358 --> 00:16:24.760
and this is enough to deal with.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.090
And so this dashed line
will represent that ideal.

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:32.260
If you remember from the
watermarks slide I had earlier,

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:36.022
and you can see where these
relate relative to that ideal.

00:16:36.022 --> 00:16:38.733
This score of three is really
close-- relatively close to it.

00:16:38.733 --> 00:16:41.001
So it happened around--

00:16:41.001 --> 00:16:41.500
what is it?

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.930
I can't tell from
here-- almost 12:07,

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:46.060
and it arrived not too
shortly thereafter.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.250
But then you've got
this nine example.

00:16:48.250 --> 00:16:52.166
This one actually
showed up quite a while

00:16:52.166 --> 00:16:53.540
after the event
actually happens.

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:57.605
So you can see there's that much
greater skew from the ideal.

00:16:57.605 --> 00:16:59.980
And so we're going to look at
a few different animations.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:02.020
So this is just the
initial pipeline

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.869
we talked about where we read in
data and then we compute a sum.

00:17:04.869 --> 00:17:07.661
And so what's happening here is
there's this thick, thick line

00:17:07.661 --> 00:17:08.619
coming from the bottom.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:11.480
And the animation just loops, so
you can watch it over and over.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.599
This thick, white line
ascending up the bottom

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:16.569
represents time as we observe
it as the pipeline observes

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:17.480
these data coming in.

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:18.970
So as the line reaches
one of the elements,

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:20.394
it's basically
seeing that element

00:17:20.394 --> 00:17:22.060
and incorporating it
into the sum, which

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:24.670
you can see accumulating
there in the middle.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:27.970
And once it gets to the
top, it computes its answer.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:32.260
And the rectangle turns blue,
and it materializes the result,

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.210
right?

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:36.320
So this is pretty
standard batch processing.

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:38.069
So let's make it a
little more interesting

00:17:38.069 --> 00:17:40.730
and move forward and start
answering some more questions.

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:42.710
So the first question
I want to touch on is

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:43.730
where in event time.

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:47.290
So the answer to the where
question is windowing.

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:50.200
So windowing is a way
of dividing data up

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:52.300
into event-time,
finite-based chunks.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.730
So this lets you get
your temporal chunking

00:17:54.730 --> 00:17:57.160
within the data, within
this infinite data stream,

00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:01.000
so that you can know
where you're actually

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.706
calculating within a
finite set of the data.

00:18:03.706 --> 00:18:08.050
There's a number of different
examples of types of windowing.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:09.310
I have three up here--

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:11.680
fixed windows, where you
basically just slice up

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:16.450
the data into fixed, say, hourly
windows or 10-minute windows.

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:19.060
Sliding is a lot like fixed
except you slide a little bit

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:19.670
each time.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:21.490
And so the interesting
thing there

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:23.290
is that the windows
start to overlap.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.342
And sessions are a very
interesting example

00:18:26.342 --> 00:18:28.300
of windowing that we
won't go into detail here.

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:30.567
But basically, they're based
of the data themselves.

00:18:30.567 --> 00:18:32.650
So you can't know ahead
of time what sessions are.

00:18:32.650 --> 00:18:35.650
They actually try to capture
some quality of the data

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:36.150
itself.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:39.070
So usually it's defined as
you see a sequence of activity

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:41.210
for a given key, like
for a given user,

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:44.050
they're active on your
website and you group them

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.457
into groups of activity
that then terminate

00:18:46.457 --> 00:18:49.040
when the user stops interacting
with your service or whatever.

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.414
So you can see these bursts
of activity of a user,

00:18:52.414 --> 00:18:54.580
and they allow you to reason
about engagement levels

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:55.610
and things like that.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:19:01.080
So they're very powerful
and very interesting.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:04.960
But let's go ahead and add some
windowing to our pipeline here.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:10.000
So in order to do that in Beam,
you just simply add this window

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:10.520
transform.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.240
And it reads quite plainly--

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:16.360
window into fix windows
of two minutes long.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:19.300
And so if we then go back
to our animated example,

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:22.000
again running this
on a batch system,

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.155
you can see instead of
accumulating a single result,

00:19:25.155 --> 00:19:28.510
we've now sliced it up
into two-minute slices

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:29.379
of event time.

00:19:29.379 --> 00:19:31.920
So in this case, we're looking
at about nine minutes of data.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.250
So we end up with
four different scores

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:36.662
across four two-minute windows.

00:19:36.662 --> 00:19:38.620
And again, as before,
this is a batch pipeline,

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:40.286
so we wait until we've
seen all the data

00:19:40.286 --> 00:19:44.470
and get our results at the end.

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:45.950
But the whole point
of this talk is

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:47.110
this talk about
being able to switch

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:48.276
between batch and streaming.

00:19:48.276 --> 00:19:50.470
So let's see what it takes,
then, to then take this

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:53.816
and move it into streaming.

00:19:53.816 --> 00:19:55.440
So once we want to
move into streaming,

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:57.640
we can't wait until we've
seen all of the input

00:19:57.640 --> 00:19:59.770
in order to produce
results so we've

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:03.440
got to somehow figure out
a way of producing results

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:04.220
earlier than that.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:05.360
And that's when we
start to answer the

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:07.070
when question-- when
in processing time

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:08.922
do we materialize these results?

00:20:08.922 --> 00:20:10.880
And we do that with this
thing called triggers.

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.820
So triggers control when
results are emitted.

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:15.872
So what we'll do
to begin with is

00:20:15.872 --> 00:20:17.330
we'll add a trigger
that says we're

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:19.730
going to trigger when
the watermark passes

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:20.900
the end of the window.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:22.611
This is effectively
the default trigger,

00:20:22.611 --> 00:20:24.110
so we wouldn't need
to specify this.

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:26.520
You could actually use the
code from the previous example

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.716
in a streaming pipeline
and get this effect.

00:20:28.716 --> 00:20:30.590
And this is also,
effectively, the same thing

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:33.630
that's happening when
you run in batch mode.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:35.780
But if we do this in
a streaming example,

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:40.101
then, we'll look first here at
a perfect watermark example.

00:20:40.101 --> 00:20:41.600
So in this case,
we have a watermark

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:44.152
represented by that
dashed screen line, which

00:20:44.152 --> 00:20:45.110
is a perfect watermark.

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:48.260
So it's going to guarantee us
that we never see late data.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.270
And basically what
happens is the system

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:53.160
waits until the watermark
passes the end of the window.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:55.070
And once it does,
it knows, OK, I'm

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:57.380
never going to see any
more data for this window,

00:20:57.380 --> 00:21:00.020
so it's OK to go ahead and
materialize the result for it.

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:02.655
So you see, as before, we
get our four results of 14,

00:21:02.655 --> 00:21:06.770
22 3, and 12, but it does so
in this incremental fashion

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:09.560
as the data for those
windows become complete.

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:13.250
And in this way, allows
you to continually process

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.290
an unbounded stream of
data and give results

00:21:15.290 --> 00:21:17.930
that come out as they go.

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:21.320
Now let's look at another
example here next to it

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:22.580
of a heuristic watermark.

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:25.000
So let's imagine we have a
watermark that's not perfect.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.230
And as you can see, it looks
different than the dashed one.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:31.040
This solid green watermark
is a little more aggressive

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:32.800
and it actually gets it wrong.

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:36.010
It doesn't know about that nine
that ends up showing up late.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.919
And so what happens here
is that time progresses on.

00:21:38.919 --> 00:21:40.460
And we emit our
windows as they come.

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:42.339
But in that first
window, it thought

00:21:42.339 --> 00:21:43.880
it was complete when
it saw the five,

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.590
and the watermark advanced.

00:21:45.590 --> 00:21:48.370
And then later,
along came the nine,

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:50.550
and there's nothing the
system could do about it.

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:53.050
It had already produced results,
so we got the wrong answer.

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:53.670
We said five.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:55.510
The answer should
have actually been 14.

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:58.220
So how can we deal with that?

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:01.120
So triggers provide
ways to deal with this.

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:03.655
So in this case we
can make our trigger

00:22:03.655 --> 00:22:07.330
a little more complex to
deal with the subtleties

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:09.200
of our specific data set.

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:12.190
So in this case,
we've added two new--

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:14.530
two modifications to
the watermark trigger.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:17.530
We've said, well, we want
early firings every minute

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:18.770
of processing time.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:21.730
So this is basically going to
give us speculative results

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.200
as our data evolve.

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:25.240
And then, also, to deal
with things like that

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.115
late nine that showed
up, we're going to say,

00:22:27.115 --> 00:22:28.990
if we ever get late
data, we're going

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:33.921
to go ahead and trigger a
firing again anytime we see one.

00:22:33.921 --> 00:22:35.420
We don't expect a
whole lot of data.

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:38.680
So we'll just do it anytime we
see an element count of one.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:41.590
And that way, we will account
for these late records showing

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:42.400
up.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:43.980
So if we then use
this trigger, we

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:47.230
can see that our two
examples now look like this.

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:50.410
The nice thing here is that
in the heuristic watermark

00:22:50.410 --> 00:22:53.819
case, when that late nine shows
up, you can see we go ahead

00:22:53.819 --> 00:22:54.610
and incorporate it.

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:56.980
And it gets incorporated
into a late result.

00:22:56.980 --> 00:23:00.220
And each of the panes here is
annotated with whether it's

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:03.362
an early result are an on-time
results or late results.

00:23:03.362 --> 00:23:05.320
Another interesting
feature of this you can see

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:10.030
is that it helps with that
too slow problem of watermarks

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:11.240
that we talked about earlier.

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:13.510
So without the
early watermark, you

00:23:13.510 --> 00:23:16.990
had to wait almost
10 minutes to get

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:19.750
an answer of that first window
in the perfect watermark case.

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:21.760
Even though you knew it
was at least five early

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:24.730
on, whereas with this additional
early trigger that we put

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:27.310
in here, you start to get these
speculative results to let

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:31.117
you see your results
build up over time.

00:23:31.117 --> 00:23:32.950
So that brings us to
the last question here.

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:34.158
So how do refinements relate?

00:23:34.158 --> 00:23:37.620
When we start having multiple
panes within a window,

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:40.302
how did those panes
relate to each other?

00:23:40.302 --> 00:23:42.010
So we're going to look
through an example

00:23:42.010 --> 00:23:44.460
here where we're going
to have three trigger

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.450
firings, a speculative
firing, a watermark

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:47.914
firing, and a late firing.

00:23:47.914 --> 00:23:49.330
And for each of
those, you can see

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:50.579
which elements have showed up.

00:23:50.579 --> 00:23:53.356
So the speculative firing
will include a value of three.

00:23:53.356 --> 00:23:55.480
The watermark firing will
include a five and a one.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:58.486
And then we'll get a late
firing with a value of two.

00:23:58.486 --> 00:24:00.610
And we'll look at each of
the three different modes

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:03.070
that we have here to
deal with defining

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:04.540
how refinements relate.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.494
So the first one
is discarding mode.

00:24:06.494 --> 00:24:08.410
Or another way to think
of it is a delta mode.

00:24:08.410 --> 00:24:12.550
So when the trigger
fires, we throw away

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:15.340
whatever state we've built up,
whatever sum we've accumulated,

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:17.410
and start over again from zero.

00:24:17.410 --> 00:24:19.170
And so you end up
with these panes--

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:20.320
three, six, and two--

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:23.917
that don't really relate
to each other in any way.

00:24:23.917 --> 00:24:26.500
In the last observed value, it
has nothing to do with the sum,

00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:28.180
even though we're going
to be computing sums.

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:29.680
But if you sum them
up individually,

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:31.067
you do get the final sum.

00:24:31.067 --> 00:24:33.400
This is useful for cases where
your outputs are actually

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:36.675
going into another system that's
doing some further aggregation.

00:24:36.675 --> 00:24:38.800
The second one, which is
what we've seen all along,

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:43.269
is accumulating mode, which
actually holds onto that state

00:24:43.269 --> 00:24:43.810
that you see.

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:45.726
You can think of this
as, maybe, a value mode.

00:24:45.726 --> 00:24:47.680
It gives you the current
value every time.

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:50.110
So we see three first time,
then five and one show up.

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:52.700
You add those together, and the
next time the value is nine.

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.220
So every time, you
can just replace

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:59.929
the previous value with the new
value to get what is current.

00:24:59.929 --> 00:25:01.720
And so that's why you
see the last observed

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.015
there is 11 at the end.

00:25:03.015 --> 00:25:04.390
It gives you that
correct result.

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:07.720
The downside with this
system is if you actually

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:10.294
sum those up themselves,
you double count.

00:25:10.294 --> 00:25:11.710
So you have to
make sure that this

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.450
is used in a system
where you can overwrite

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:14.825
previous values
where there's not

00:25:14.825 --> 00:25:18.260
going to be any accumulation.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:21.040
The one you really want, which
we don't have quite implemented

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:24.440
yet, but is accumulating
and retracting mode,

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:26.530
which gives you, essentially,
accumulating mode,

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:28.075
but also every time it
gives you a new value,

00:25:28.075 --> 00:25:30.116
it gives you a retraction
for the previous value.

00:25:30.116 --> 00:25:33.350
It says, with that
on-time firing, it says,

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:35.860
well, previously I'd
said the value was three.

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:38.470
Well, take that away,
retract that, and also

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:40.090
here's the new value of nine.

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:41.800
So that allows you
to, in situations

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:45.280
where there may be further
aggregations down the pipeline,

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:48.037
you can actually get the
correct result that way.

00:25:48.037 --> 00:25:50.620
Or if you just look at the last
observed non-retraction value,

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:54.560
you also get the correct result.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:58.390
So we'll look real quickly at
what adding a retracting mode

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:00.930
does to the pipeline.

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:03.390
So this is our same
example from before.

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:07.130
But now every time we
fire a non-initial pane,

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:08.190
you see we get two panes.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.850
We get both the new
result in yellow and then

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:13.800
also this retraction
in red that's saying,

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:15.550
the previous value
that I told you before,

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.620
well, go ahead and get rid
of that, because I was wrong.

00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:22.670
And that allows you to really
properly update results

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:25.460
over time, even without having
to just blindly overwrite

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:28.100
a previous value.

00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:30.650
All right, so before
I hand off to Loic

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:33.650
to hear about a more
concrete example of this,

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:37.580
I want to talk very quickly
about how all this enables

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:39.890
batch and streaming interop.

00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:41.840
So it really comes
down to two things that

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:43.610
are provided by the system--

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:45.770
this ability for
correctness in light

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:48.130
of changing inputs or
different types of inputs

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:50.870
and in different ways of
processing those inputs

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:52.670
under the covers, and
then the flexibility

00:26:52.670 --> 00:26:55.490
afforded by the model
to make those choices.

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:59.450
So on the correctness
front, we've

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:02.480
talked about distributed systems
and how they're distributed

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:05.360
and you never know what
you're going to get.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.565
So to really
quantify this for us,

00:27:07.565 --> 00:27:09.966
let's look at two
examples of how we might

00:27:09.966 --> 00:27:11.090
have observed these events.

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:12.881
So you can see they're
shifting up and down

00:27:12.881 --> 00:27:15.810
between this white set of input
records and this purple set.

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:18.060
You'll notice that they
aren't shifting on the x-axis.

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:19.159
These are the same events.

00:27:19.159 --> 00:27:20.450
They happened at the same time.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.790
We're just changing the order
in which we observe them

00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:23.623
within the pipeline.

00:27:23.623 --> 00:27:26.400
We're changing their
processing time.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:29.870
So if we were to process these
records using processing time

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:32.150
windows where you just
view them as they come in,

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:34.650
what effectively is happening
is you're taking those events,

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:36.260
you're throwing
away the event time,

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:38.259
and you're shifting
them onto the ideal.

00:27:38.259 --> 00:27:39.800
You're saying, when
I saw this event,

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:42.049
I'm going to treat that as
its position in the window.

00:27:42.049 --> 00:27:48.030
So you're-- I seem to have--
you're mapping processing time

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:50.160
onto event time and
then summing things up.

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.080
And you can see when
you change the ordering,

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:54.462
then you get different results.

00:27:54.462 --> 00:27:55.920
So the first window
in order number

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:57.564
one, you get a result of 12.

00:27:57.564 --> 00:27:58.980
And ordering two,
you get a result

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:01.020
of seven, which
isn't what you want.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:04.800
Whereas if you use event time
windowing, where you actually

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.360
aggregate and
process these events

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:09.455
within the event-time
windows when they happened,

00:28:09.455 --> 00:28:10.830
you get the same
result no matter

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:12.390
what the processing
time ordering.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:15.102
Now the actual shape of results
over time as they evolve

00:28:15.102 --> 00:28:15.810
may be different.

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:19.310
And that's just a characteristic
of how your data arrive.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:22.080
But at the end of the day, you
come up with the same results.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.990
And this is a key part of how
you can provide a system that

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:27.450
operates the same even
if it's in streaming mode

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:29.500
or in batch mode.

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:31.980
The second part is flexibility.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:35.130
So the five examples
we've walked through,

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:36.630
you can see how the
system gives you

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:38.520
the flexibility to
choose the ones that

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:39.540
work for your use case.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:41.040
And specifically,
when we're talking

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.260
about switching between
batch and streaming,

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.480
there were three cases
there that they gave me

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:48.630
the same final results no
matter whether it was batch mode

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:49.996
or in streaming mode.

00:28:49.996 --> 00:28:52.620
And if you don't care about late
data, really, there were four.

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:59.379
And we did this with
relatively minor code changes.

00:28:59.379 --> 00:29:01.170
I don't expect you to
be able to read this.

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:03.480
But the point is
that we didn't make

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:06.120
a whole lot of different changes
to fit these different use

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:06.720
cases.

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:08.670
And the really
important point is

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:11.640
that we never changed
our core algorithm,

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:13.090
that sum of integers per key.

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:16.560
And this is true no matter
how complex your algorithm is.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:18.810
You define what it
is you're computing,

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:22.140
and then everything else you may
need to change in slight ways

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:23.760
to tweak it for
batch or streaming,

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:27.060
is totally independent of
that, which really gives you

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:30.120
the flexibility to cleanly
switch between the two.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:33.660
So I've now given you the
theoretical, conceptual

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:36.030
foundation for why we can
support this switching

00:29:36.030 --> 00:29:37.305
between batch and streaming.

00:29:37.305 --> 00:29:39.150
I'm going to now
hand off to Loic,

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:41.790
who's going to give you a little
more concrete details of how

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:45.022
they've done that with
their system at Teads.

00:29:45.022 --> 00:29:45.980
LOIC JAURES: Thank you.

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:48.220
Thank you, Tyler.

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:50.180
So yeah, you're right.

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:52.750
The proposal of
my talk today was

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:55.000
going to be to
give you a feedback

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:59.590
of our usage of Dataflow
but also BigQuery

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:03.070
and more broadly about GCP.

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:05.740
So as I mentioned
in the intro, we'll

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:09.130
start by talking
a bit about Teads.

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:13.580
So we created Teads in 2011.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:19.420
And we now have 27 offices
in 21 countries in the world.

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:24.340
Last year we achieved
200 million of revenues.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:28.570
We have 500 employees, and
among them, 90 plus of them

00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:30.070
are from the R&amp;D team.

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:39.080
And what we do, we try to solve,
measure issue this advertising

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:42.070
industry has, which is the
scarcity of premium video

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:43.280
inventory.

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:47.390
Only 5% of online video
inventory is premium.

00:30:47.390 --> 00:30:52.890
So we've created a
format to fix this issue.

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:59.414
So I have a quick video to show
you, the formats in action.

00:31:02.732 --> 00:31:05.320
All right, OK.

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:08.830
So this is the inRead.

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:12.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:18.875
So you probably came
across this format.

00:31:18.875 --> 00:31:20.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:25.250
So it's a way to embed video
within editorial content.

00:31:25.250 --> 00:31:28.290
So as you can see, the video
is paused when it's not visible

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:30.730
and displaying only
when it is visible.

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:34.960
And you can tap on the ad to see
it full screen with sound on.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:38.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:52.030
OK, so let's move on
to the next slide.

00:31:52.030 --> 00:31:55.060
With this simple
format, in fact,

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:57.790
we've been able to solve
issue for the three

00:31:57.790 --> 00:31:59.930
main actors of this industry.

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:01.600
So first the publishers.

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:04.870
There's a lot of
publishers that they

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:08.130
have a lot of textual content.

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:11.350
And they want to monetize
this content through video.

00:32:11.350 --> 00:32:16.750
So with this format from
one day to the other one,

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:20.320
they could monetize all
that content with video.

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:22.850
For advertisers,
they want to have

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:27.520
viewable ad, which is what's
the inRead does by design.

00:32:27.520 --> 00:32:32.560
And they want to be displayed
within premium content, which

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:35.380
we bring with the
publishers that we have.

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:38.530
And finally the
publishers-- the users.

00:32:38.530 --> 00:32:41.980
We try to create the
formats in such a way which

00:32:41.980 --> 00:32:47.020
is not intrusive, which
is engaging for our user.

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:52.150
And this way, you can skip the
ad if you're not interested.

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:55.200
Or you can activate a
song if you are interested

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:58.330
and only if you are
interested in the ad.

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:03.790
It's not working.

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:11.710
So today we are the number one
video marketplace in the world.

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:17.350
And we can see on this graph
the growth that we have.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:23.710
During the past years,
starting in 2012 until now,

00:33:23.710 --> 00:33:28.010
today we reach more than 1.2
billion users every month.

00:33:28.010 --> 00:33:33.400
So we're also within
the club of websites

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:36.700
reaching more than
one billion users.

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:40.070
So the question is what do
we do with this kind of data?

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:42.160
So nothing fancy here.

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:45.760
We do BI for internal purpose.

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.130
We do, also, analytics.

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:53.530
So we provide publishers and
advertisers with a dashboard

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:55.330
so they can understand
their website.

00:33:55.330 --> 00:33:58.480
They can understand how
their ads are performing.

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:06.090
We optimize our algorithm
through machine learning.

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:08.440
And I have few of
those use cases.

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:13.239
But the last one I would like
to mention is fraud detection.

00:34:13.239 --> 00:34:18.340
So we started in 2012,
and as Tyler mentioned,

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:24.170
back then the code from
Nathan Marz was the reference.

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:30.949
And basically Nathan is
explaining in these codes

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:33.800
that the system has
two main features.

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:35.530
The first one is the storage.

00:34:35.530 --> 00:34:38.020
We need to ingest all the data.

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:42.489
And the second one is the
way we retrieve and bring

00:34:42.489 --> 00:34:44.270
the result to the end users.

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:47.040
But we have to keep in mind
that this kind of system

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:50.320
of a lifetime, which
is pretty long.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:56.030
So for us, our system is running
for six years and even more.

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:58.720
And therefore, we
need to find a way

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:04.540
to overcome any kind of hardware
failure that could occur.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:08.980
Any kind of human mistakes, even
though we try to avoid them,

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:11.900
sometimes it happens.

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:16.510
So that's why it's really
crucial to store raw data.

00:35:16.510 --> 00:35:20.470
And once you have raw
data, you can simply

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:22.720
apply a function to it.

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:24.700
And you can have
your end result.

00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:27.670
So this is perfect,
but the issue

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:32.150
is that all data means
a few petabytes of data.

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:35.380
So it's not applicable
in real life.

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:38.310
So to solve this issue,
you need to create

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:45.280
some views, pre-computed views,
to accelerate the queries.

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:49.720
So every time you have a new
use case, a new client asking

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:52.480
for something, you need
to create a new view

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:56.860
to support this new use case.

00:35:56.860 --> 00:36:02.300
So we ended up operating and
managing Lambda architecture.

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:06.310
So this is Lambda
architecture by the book.

00:36:06.310 --> 00:36:10.570
And for us, we are using--

00:36:10.570 --> 00:36:12.640
we were using
Amazon Web Services.

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:17.260
So we are using Kafka
for the queue messaging.

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:20.360
Then the data as two ways.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:24.460
The first one for the batch
layer, so the first step

00:36:24.460 --> 00:36:27.970
is to store the data
so we can be safe.

00:36:27.970 --> 00:36:30.560
And the data is there.

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:33.310
So for that, we use what
we call the fetcher,

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:36.070
and we store data on S3.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:41.380
And then the batch mode is
computing all these views

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:43.570
on these rollups.

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:46.660
And we used to do
it in MapReduce,

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:49.180
then we moved to Spark.

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:53.590
And we store the end
result in Cassandra.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:58.600
On the bottom of these charts,
you have the speed layer.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:02.030
So for that, we use Storm.

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:06.460
And once again the data
is outputted in Cassandra.

00:37:06.460 --> 00:37:10.700
And for the final result,
we are using a tool

00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:12.410
that we call reporting.

00:37:12.410 --> 00:37:13.690
That's merged in the data.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:17.020
So if the data is
available in batch mode,

00:37:17.020 --> 00:37:18.580
we are taking this one.

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:21.910
Otherwise we offer backing on
the real-time data that has

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:24.070
been computed through Storm.

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:31.930
So this kind of
architecture is pretty great

00:37:31.930 --> 00:37:35.290
because you can fix
any kind of issues.

00:37:35.290 --> 00:37:39.770
If you have stored the data, you
can recreate it from scratch.

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:43.060
So you just discard
the batch data.

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:46.930
And then use the raw data
to compute the new views.

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:48.760
And you're fine again.

00:37:48.760 --> 00:37:50.740
You fix the issues.

00:37:50.740 --> 00:37:54.850
And cherry on the cake, you
can even create view for needs

00:37:54.850 --> 00:37:59.230
you didn't think of
previously because you

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:03.060
have the data to support this.

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:05.570
Few numbers.

00:38:05.570 --> 00:38:08.090
So I should have mentioned
that we are using

00:38:08.090 --> 00:38:12.410
Hadoop for both the Spark jobs.

00:38:12.410 --> 00:38:17.150
And then to use the reporting,
so there's two [? uses ?]

00:38:17.150 --> 00:38:18.950
for the Hadoop cluster.

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:23.570
We have a Hadoop cluster
of around 120 nodes.

00:38:23.570 --> 00:38:27.050
Our Cassandra cluster
is around 60 nodes.

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:33.420
And we ingest, everyday,
around 4 terabytes of data.

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:37.560
And this is done only with
a team of three people.

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:43.680
So with this kind of scale,
you start to face limitations.

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:45.960
So just to name a few.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:47.940
The number of views
you need to create

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:53.490
to support all your client
needs, then to grow very fast,

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:55.980
so you have to
create a lot of them.

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:58.890
And therefore, when
there's a mistake,

00:38:58.890 --> 00:39:01.260
and you need to recompute
all those views,

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:03.870
it's quite expensive.

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:09.690
You also need to create
some internal jobs to add

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:12.700
some steps to bring
the end result.

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:17.250
And therefore you ended up
with a lot of complexity.

00:39:17.250 --> 00:39:20.160
And finally, not the
least, because you

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:25.500
are managing those big clusters,
it involves a lot of work

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:29.520
in terms of monitoring,
automation, and so on.

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:32.850
So it doesn't scale magically.

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:38.040
So we started to think about
an ideal solution for us, which

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:43.180
would be using only one
single unified view.

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:46.440
And therefore with
this view, we could

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:48.870
have all the
combinations possible

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:53.040
and combine all the
dimensions with bits.

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:56.640
We wanted it to be
really fast to be

00:39:56.640 --> 00:40:00.720
able to compute those
views in minutes, not days.

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:04.830
And finally, if possible,
it would be manageable,

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:07.690
more manageable than
the previous system.

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:13.050
And why not managed by someone
else, if you see what I mean.

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:19.710
So that's why we tried to
run a POC with BigQuery.

00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:23.520
So as our data are
were lying on the S3,

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:30.210
we just transferred the data
to GCP using data transfer.

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:34.170
And then, because at the time
the packet was not supported,

00:40:34.170 --> 00:40:40.350
we added a step with Dataproc
to convert data and ingest

00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:42.210
the data in the query.

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:46.290
So since then, packet
support has been developed.

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:51.050
So it will be easier
if I to do is it now.

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:54.200
Once you have the data, you
can try to play with it.

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:59.630
And to create this unified
view, we were eager to have.

00:40:59.630 --> 00:41:04.580
So this is how the data looks
like when we try to ingest it.

00:41:04.580 --> 00:41:09.940
There's basically a
timestamp and a few IDs.

00:41:09.940 --> 00:41:14.780
So advertisement ID, website ID.

00:41:14.780 --> 00:41:18.080
There's tons of them.

00:41:18.080 --> 00:41:21.050
And finally, there's the event.

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:24.830
So the first thing we can do
is try to compress the data,

00:41:24.830 --> 00:41:28.250
because we do not provide
reports per minute

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:29.840
but per hour.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:32.360
So we could get rid
of the timestamped

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:37.330
and replace it by the
hour plus a counter.

00:41:37.330 --> 00:41:42.130
So this is the first
step, which is fine.

00:41:42.130 --> 00:41:46.180
But we can go even deeper.

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:51.100
Something we want to do is
to use a dedicated column

00:41:51.100 --> 00:41:54.240
for every kind of
event that we can have.

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:57.660
So have a column for
impression, a column for start,

00:41:57.660 --> 00:42:01.600
and finally, there's
one for clicks.

00:42:01.600 --> 00:42:06.250
And this way, we can go from
four rows in the first example

00:42:06.250 --> 00:42:12.100
to only one with three counters,
two impressions, one start,

00:42:12.100 --> 00:42:12.940
and one click.

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:22.920
And you can leverage the
columnar storage system

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:28.270
of BigQuery, because if you
want to query only impressions,

00:42:28.270 --> 00:42:31.510
you can just read
those integers instead

00:42:31.510 --> 00:42:37.824
of having to scroll and read
a string, which is way faster.

00:42:37.824 --> 00:42:41.120
And as the data
is quite similar,

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:43.330
it's a good use case
for compression.

00:42:43.330 --> 00:42:46.150
So this way, we've been
[INAUDIBLE] to compressed data

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:49.090
by 70x.

00:42:49.090 --> 00:42:51.880
And we try to run query to it.

00:42:51.880 --> 00:42:55.530
So we've been impressed by
the performance of BigQuery.

00:42:55.530 --> 00:43:00.790
And we, in fact, achieved better
results and better performance

00:43:00.790 --> 00:43:05.140
on this hourly rollup
than the previous system,

00:43:05.140 --> 00:43:07.750
even though it was
optimized for query.

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:10.560
So with this
success, we moved on

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:15.030
to the next step, which
involved Dataflow, which is

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:18.280
the live ingestion of the data.

00:43:18.280 --> 00:43:23.020
So as we have in this
queue messaging, Kafka,

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.900
we just plugged Dataflow to it.

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:28.870
So keep in mind that we are
using two different cloud

00:43:28.870 --> 00:43:30.190
providers.

00:43:30.190 --> 00:43:34.600
So Dataflow is responsible
to retrieve the data to a AWS

00:43:34.600 --> 00:43:40.370
from AWS and to
insert it in BigQuery.

00:43:40.370 --> 00:43:43.300
So it was really,
really easy to do

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:48.280
because we perform very few
modifications on the data,

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:50.260
and it was working fine.

00:43:50.260 --> 00:43:54.980
But there's a few challenges
that we would like to discuss.

00:43:54.980 --> 00:43:59.030
The first one is, I
think, pretty obvious.

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:02.030
I think two clouds adds
a bit of complexity.

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:03.500
So we need to handle this.

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:08.080
We need to avoid creating
the same stuff plenty times.

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:10.420
So for instance, we
have all your rights

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:13.960
on one cloud provider and the
same one on the second one.

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:16.990
That needs to be kept in sync.

00:44:16.990 --> 00:44:20.170
Same goes for network
configuration and so on.

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:24.490
So we are investing
a lot in Terraform.

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:29.290
And I think you should do the
same if even though you only

00:44:29.290 --> 00:44:34.940
operate on one cloud provider,
its a good use of your time.

00:44:37.892 --> 00:44:43.170
And second issue
that we faced is not

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:46.360
really linked to Dataflow
but more to BigQuery.

00:44:46.360 --> 00:44:50.760
So Dataflow is really
fast and pretty cheap,

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:53.940
but when you are trying
to ingest raw data, which

00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:57.990
means more terabytes per day,
the streaming mode of BigQuery

00:44:57.990 --> 00:45:00.120
could be a bit expensive.

00:45:00.120 --> 00:45:02.880
And we start to think
about those questions

00:45:02.880 --> 00:45:03.910
that we mentioned.

00:45:03.910 --> 00:45:06.390
Do we need to go
to stream or batch?

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:11.130
And we acknowledged that
it was not the right use

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:12.930
case for the streaming mode.

00:45:12.930 --> 00:45:17.460
So we switched to what
we call the micro batch,

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:21.130
meaning batched
every 15 minutes,

00:45:21.130 --> 00:45:23.113
which is fine for our use case.

00:45:23.113 --> 00:45:26.370
And we also think about
all the solutions,

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:30.540
like sampling the data when it's
technical metrics when you just

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:34.900
need the trend and not exact
value and this kind of stuff.

00:45:34.900 --> 00:45:39.270
So this is the end result.
You have your timeline

00:45:39.270 --> 00:45:41.100
with the events.

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:45.340
The color is representing
the hour of [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:50.490
So for us, its mostly
[? ordered fine, ?]

00:45:50.490 --> 00:45:54.080
but you could have late events.

00:45:54.080 --> 00:45:57.060
And we just read
a batch of data,

00:45:57.060 --> 00:46:03.090
a chunk of data using Dataflow
and write it to BigQuery.

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:07.140
So without a lot
of modifications,

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:11.860
just a few checks, and casting.

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:18.100
And then we are
leveraging the SQL power,

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:21.740
and we create those
rollups I mentioned before.

00:46:21.740 --> 00:46:25.210
And we are separating the
data out from the hour of 7:00

00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:27.760
AM from 8:00 AM.

00:46:27.760 --> 00:46:31.900
And we write those in
two different tables.

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:34.450
And then, as time
goes by, there's

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:37.150
new events that need
to be processed.

00:46:37.150 --> 00:46:42.790
So first step, ingestion
on BigQuery using Dataflow.

00:46:42.790 --> 00:46:46.340
And then we append the data.

00:46:46.340 --> 00:46:49.960
And you can see.

00:46:49.960 --> 00:46:53.660
I guess you've
understood the principle.

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:59.710
OK, so this is the overview
and the final architecture.

00:46:59.710 --> 00:47:06.590
So Kafka, Dataflow consuming the
data, writing it to BigQuery,

00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:11.330
then applying SQL query
to generate those rollups.

00:47:11.330 --> 00:47:14.870
And we can go even
deeper with new queries

00:47:14.870 --> 00:47:19.670
to create specialized data
marts for specific needs in case

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:20.330
we need those.

00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:27.050
So I just want to go back.

00:47:27.050 --> 00:47:31.310
I mentioned this before,
the fraud detections.

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:36.430
So right now, we have a
loop, which is every hour.

00:47:36.430 --> 00:47:38.530
We are retrieving the data.

00:47:38.530 --> 00:47:40.690
We are checking
to see if there's

00:47:40.690 --> 00:47:43.160
any fraud or anomaly detections.

00:47:43.160 --> 00:47:45.400
And we think this is
a perfect use case

00:47:45.400 --> 00:47:47.520
to leverage the streaming
mode of Dataflow

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:55.172
and try to speed up a bit this
feedback loop to have faster

00:47:55.172 --> 00:47:55.880
feedback results.

00:47:58.640 --> 00:48:03.080
So in terms of
results, we've been

00:48:03.080 --> 00:48:06.530
able to shrink our cluster.

00:48:06.530 --> 00:48:10.640
So the Hadoop cluster, or
the equivalent of Hadoop,

00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:15.350
goes from 120 nodes to 32 nodes.

00:48:15.350 --> 00:48:18.970
We no longer need
Cassandra, and we are using

00:48:18.970 --> 00:48:21.060
BigQuery, which is managed.

00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:23.570
So no nodes to manage.

00:48:23.570 --> 00:48:26.450
You can see there's
more data on BigQuery

00:48:26.450 --> 00:48:32.180
because we do no longer need
to discard the raw data.

00:48:32.180 --> 00:48:37.680
And we can keep it on BigQuery
and use it when we need it.

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:38.660
Volume's the same.

00:48:38.660 --> 00:48:40.460
Team size is the same.

00:48:40.460 --> 00:48:46.680
And the POC plus the PILOT has
been done in only four months.

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:53.560
So a few more slides.

00:48:53.560 --> 00:48:56.470
In terms of scale, we
were reaching the limits.

00:48:56.470 --> 00:48:59.770
We were wondering if it
was possible to scale

00:48:59.770 --> 00:49:04.090
to the next level because
it took us around 40 minutes

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:06.170
to compute one hour of data.

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:11.310
So we were wondering if
it was possible to scale.

00:49:11.310 --> 00:49:14.050
And now we know for
sure that's the case

00:49:14.050 --> 00:49:17.020
because we tried with
10 times more data,

00:49:17.020 --> 00:49:19.090
and it's working fine.

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:22.605
And in fact, the times
to process one hour data

00:49:22.605 --> 00:49:25.450
have been down to
only four minutes.

00:49:25.450 --> 00:49:29.200
So full batch of one
minute, four minutes,

00:49:29.200 --> 00:49:31.300
which is impressive.

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:35.490
In terms of costs, it used to
require a lot of maintenance

00:49:35.490 --> 00:49:38.782
to handle all
those big clusters.

00:49:38.782 --> 00:49:43.210
And it was pretty expensive.

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:46.630
So now we've delegated,
basically, all these jobs

00:49:46.630 --> 00:49:49.570
to BigQuery and to Google.

00:49:49.570 --> 00:49:51.040
So it's managed for us.

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:57.480
So the cost of operations
have been down close to zero.

00:49:57.480 --> 00:50:03.046
And we're all now under
control regarding the cost.

00:50:03.046 --> 00:50:04.420
Something I would
like to mention

00:50:04.420 --> 00:50:12.550
is that the cost been reduced
even though we have around 20%

00:50:12.550 --> 00:50:15.650
of the overall cost,
which is the egress

00:50:15.650 --> 00:50:19.900
to transfer the data from
one cloud to the other one.

00:50:19.900 --> 00:50:24.830
So there's room
for optimisation.

00:50:24.830 --> 00:50:27.290
In terms of complexity,
instead of having

00:50:27.290 --> 00:50:35.810
to chain all those long jobs
and manage a role and retries,

00:50:35.810 --> 00:50:38.100
we just have only two steps--

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:42.170
ingest data and do the rollups.

00:50:42.170 --> 00:50:46.020
And the data is now actionable
because it no longer

00:50:46.020 --> 00:50:52.080
requires you to write Spark jobs
or complicated stuff like that.

00:50:52.080 --> 00:50:54.800
You can just leverage
the SQL query.

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:59.630
So basically anyone
with basic SQL knowledge

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:03.310
could leverage the
data and use it.

00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:11.060
And finally, we are
having a single rollup

00:51:11.060 --> 00:51:12.680
instead of partial rollups.

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:16.430
So we can combine everything.

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:18.410
And we can combine
all the dimensions

00:51:18.410 --> 00:51:23.910
and answer questions we were
not able to answer previously.

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:25.710
And I would like to
conclude this thought

00:51:25.710 --> 00:51:31.000
by congratulating the wonderful
team that worked on this.

00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:33.150
So this is the [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:35.010
And thank you for them.

